geted activity level) by the actual activity level.  Therefore, the debits represent actual overhead costs incurred; credits represent the applied overhead costs.  A debit balance in the Overhead Control account at the end of the period means that overhea
d
 is underapplied.  A credit balance in the Overhead Control account means that overhead is overapplied.  If the balance is considered immaterial, it is closed to Cost of Goods Sold (COGS).  Underapplied overhead is debited to COGS and credited to Overhead
 Control.  This increases COGS by the amount of underapplied overhead and restates the COGS from the normal cost to the actual cost.  Overapplied overhead is debited to Overhead Control and credited to COGS, thus reducing COGS on the income statement.}{
